
# Workspace Context — BraTS / MR-RATE Diffusion Model Project
Last updated: 2026-04-01

---

## What We're Building

A **2D slice-based diffusion model** (DDPM/DDIM) with **LoRA fine-tuning**, trained exclusively
on healthy thin-slice T1w brain MRI from the MR-RATE dataset.
**Goal:** Unsupervised anomaly detection — given a diseased slice, reconstruct the healthy
version and expose pathology as the residual `|x₀ − x̂₀|`.

---

## Anomaly Detection Pipeline (inference)

```
Diseased slice  x₀
      │
      ▼
Saliency map from ACAT classifier  →  binary mask M
      │  (rough map of "where pathology probably is")
      ▼
DDIM encode  →  x_L
      (forward noising applied only inside mask M, to noise level L)
      │
      ▼
Hybrid DDPM + DDIM decode  (t = L → 0)
      ├─ inside  M = 1  →  DDPM steps  (stochastic, free to regenerate healthy anatomy)
      └─ outside M = 0  →  DDIM steps  (deterministic, preserves healthy anatomy exactly)
      │
      ▼
Healthy reconstruction  x̂₀
      │
      ▼
Anomaly map  =  | x₀ − x̂₀ |
```

**Key inference hyperparameters:**
- `ENCODE_T` — how far to noise (250 / 500 / 750): higher = more freedom to edit pathology
- `N_DDIM_STEPS` — reverse decode steps (50 is standard)
- `smooth_sigma` — Gaussian smoothing on anomaly map (default 2.0 px)

---

## Dataset

### Healthy Brain Data — `brain_only/`
- **Source:** Hugging Face Hub `hf://buckets/rahul2001/mr-rate-processed`
- **Downloaded via:** `load_mr_rate_data.ipynb` (421 files synced)
- **Total folders:** 212 subjects (alphanumeric IDs, e.g. `4IJW45J4TC`)
- Each folder contains: `t1_brain.nii.gz`, optionally `t2_axi_brain.nii.gz`,
  `flair_brain.nii.gz`, `t2_cor_brain.nii.gz`, `brain_mask.nii.gz`

### BraTS 2020 — `BraTS2020_TrainingData/`
- 371 glioma cases, each with `_flair.nii`, `_t1.nii`, `_t1ce.nii`, `_t2.nii`, `_seg.nii`
- Volume shape: (240, 240, 155), 1 mm isotropic
- Seg labels: 0=background, 1=necrotic/non-enhancing, 2=edema, 3=enhancing tumor
- **Role:** evaluation / reference only — not in training pipeline

---

## Key Dataset Decision: Thin-Slice Only

After analysing all 210 subjects we found two fundamentally different acquisition types:

| Group | Subjects | Z-spacing | Depth range | Slices/subj (after filter) |
|-------|----------|-----------|-------------|---------------------------|
| **Thick (excluded)** | 139 | 4–7 mm | 20–30 | 14–25 |
| **Thin (used)** | 71 | 0.47–1.04 mm | 200–640 | 73–315 |

**Why exclude thick-slice:**
- Partial-volume effects make each slice look blurry / different from thin-slice
- Only 14–25 slices per subject → barely enough anatomy
- Mixing both types would train the model to handle two incompatible appearance modes
- Confirmed visually in `03_slice_thickness_analysis.ipynb`

**Thin-slice split (deterministic, seed=42, 80/10/10):**

| Split | Subjects | Usable 2D slices |
|-------|----------|-----------------|
| Train | 55 | 9,540 |
| Val | 7 | 1,125 |
| Test | 9 | 1,995 |
| **Total** | **71** | **12,660** |

**Fill rate stats (thin-slice, 15–85% Z window):**
- 17,594 candidate slices before fill filter
- Fill rate range: 0.0–45.8% (skull-stripped brain never exceeds ~50% of frame)
- Mean / median: 18.5% / 18.7%,  Std: 14.6%
- **28% of candidates dropped** by the 5% fill filter → 12,660 retained

---

## Slice Statistics (raw → usable)

```
All 210 subjects — raw axial slices         :  28,412  (100%)
  After 15%–85% Z-range filter              :  20,074  (−29.3%)
  After 5% brain-fill filter                :  15,119  (−24.7% of candidates)
  Overall retention from raw                :   53.2%

Thin-slice only (71 subjects):
  Candidate slices in 15–85% window         :  17,594
  After 5% fill filter                      :  12,660  (72.0% retained)
```

**Volume shape diversity (thin-slice):**
- 40 unique HxW dimensions across subjects — resize to 256×256 is non-trivial
- Z depth: 200–640 slices; most common XY: 256×256 and 512×512

