## Design Decisions & Rationale

| Decision | Choice | Why |
|----------|--------|-----|
| 2D vs 3D | **2D axial slices** | Lower memory, proven for medical diffusion models |
| Modality | **T1w only** | Simplest starting point; extensible to T2/FLAIR later |
| Acquisition filter | **Thin-slice only (<2 mm)** | Thick slices look fundamentally different; mixing corrupts model |
| Normalisation | **Percentile [0.5, 99.5]** | Robust to MRI intensity outliers vs min-max |
| Spatial size | **256×256** | Balances anatomical detail with compute on CPU/consumer GPU |
| Slice selection | **1 random/subject/epoch** | Free temporal augmentation; low RAM footprint |
| Augmentation | **Flip + small affine only** | Conservative — brain anatomy must stay realistic |
| Fill threshold | **5%** | Removes near-empty edge slices; drops 28% of thin-slice candidates |
| Z-range | **15%–85% of depth** | Removes top/bottom artifact-prone slices |
| Parameter efficiency | **LoRA r=4 on attention linears** | <1% trainable params, fast training, easy to swap |
| Decode strategy | **DDPM inside mask, DDIM outside** | Stochastic where we want change; deterministic where we want preservation |
| Scheduler | **DDPM train, DDIM inference** | DDIM gives 50-step decode vs 1000-step DDPM at same quality |
